#heading(level: 2)[Marriage & Land]

*Deep dive: How to use marriage to expand your realm.*

#heading(level: 3)[The Three Uses of Marriage]

Marriage in CK3 serves three distinct purposes:

1. *Alliances* --- Your spouse's family becomes your ally. A well-married daughter can bring a kingdom into your wars.
2. *Claims* --- Your spouse's claims can become your children's claims. Your children's claims can become *your* wars.
3. *Genetics* --- Congenital traits (Intelligent, Beautiful, Herculean, Fecund) stack across generations. Breed for them.

#heading(level: 3)[Matrilineal Marriage -- When and Why]

A matrilineal marriage means children belong to the *mother's* dynasty, not the father's. Use it when:

- You have only daughters and no sons (your dynasty dies otherwise)
- You want to bring a talented character into your court without losing your dynasty name
- You want to inherit land: marry your daughter matrilineally to a second son of a kingdom. Their children (your grandchildren) will have claims on that kingdom. *Then you press those claims for your dynasty.*

Remember: AI rulers rarely accept matrilineal marriage for their primary heir. Target second, third, or fourth sons.

#heading(level: 3)[Claiming Land Through Marriage]

This is the most powerful (and most overlooked) expansion mechanic:

1. Find a character with a claim on a title you want (character finder > filter: has claims)
2. Marry them into your dynasty (matrilineally if needed)
3. Have children with them
4. Your child inherits the claim
5. Press your child's claim in war
6. Your dynasty member now rules that title --- as your ally

For pressing *spouse* claims directly: your spouse must be your faith, their claim must be inheritable, and you must be willing to go to war for it.

#heading(level: 3)[Murder as an Inheritance Tool]

If the person standing between your dynasty and a title is inconvenient: murder them. Each murder in the succession line moves your target closer to inheritance. Be careful --- getting caught as a known murderer destroys your reputation and makes everyone hate you.

#heading(level: 3)[Breeding for Traits]

Congenital traits (level 1 traits like Quick, level 2 like Intelligent, level 3 like Genius) stack:

- Two parents with the same tier trait = high chance child inherits it
- Two parents with different tier traits = chance child inherits the highest tier
- Blood legacy perks increase inheritance chance
- Inbreeding increases trait inheritance but also increases negative traits (inbred trait)

The Perfect Heir build: marry Genius characters, use the Blood dynasty legacy, and over generations your dynasty becomes superhuman.

#pagebreak()

#heading(level: 2)[Succession & Dynasties]

*Deep dive: Keeping your realm together when your ruler dies.*

#heading(level: 3)[Succession Types]

Your realm splinters on death based on succession law. Understanding this is critical:

- *Confederate Partition (default):* All sons inherit equally. New titles are *automatically created* if you hold enough land to form them. This means your kingdom might split into multiple kingdoms on death.
- *Partition:* Same as Confederate, but new titles are NOT automatically created. Still splits your realm.
- *High Partition:* Your primary heir gets the majority. Secondary heirs get a smaller share.
- *Primogeniture:* Everything goes to your firstborn. Requires high crown authority and late medieval era.
- *Elective:* Various forms (Feudal, Saxon, Tanistry, Princely). Your vassals vote on the heir. You can manipulate votes but cannot fully control them.

#heading(level: 3)[Managing Partition]

Until you get Primogeniture (usually 200+ years in), Partition is your reality. Strategies:

- *Disinherit:* Costs 150 renown per child. Expensive but reliable. Disinherit all but your best heir.
- *Force vows:* Send excess sons to the clergy (if your faith allows). They can't inherit.
- *Kill excess sons:* Imprison and execute. Extremely tyrannical but effective. Everyone will hate you.
- *Grant titles before death:* Give secondary sons land *before* you die. They inherit their granted land but don't partition your core domain.
- *Conquer enough for everyone:* If you hold two kingdom titles, your secondary son becomes king of the second kingdom instead of splitting your primary.
- *Designate heir (Absolute Crown Authority):* At absolute crown authority, you can pick any child as heir. Expensive and requires late tech.

#heading(level: 3)[Dynasty Mechanics]

Your dynasty is your real identity. Key concepts:

- *Renown:* Generated by living dynasty members (especially rulers). Spent on dynasty legacies (permanent bonuses).
- *Legacies:* Blood (traits), Guile (intrigue), Kin (family), Glory (war), Law (stewardship). Blood is the most popular first tree.
- *Cadet branches:* When a dynasty member becomes powerful enough, they can form a cadet branch. Independent cadet branches generate renown for the main dynasty.
- *Dynasty head:* The most powerful member of the dynasty by realm size. Can legitimize bastards, disinherit, and call dynasty members to war.

#heading(level: 3)[House Unity]

With the latest DLC, house unity affects realm stability. Keep your house members happy through grants, marriages, and sway schemes. Low house unity means more factions, more murder plots, and less tax income from dynasty members.

#pagebreak()

#heading(level: 2)[Religion]

*Deep dive: Faith as a weapon.*

#heading(level: 3)[Faith Mechanics]

Your faith determines:
- What's considered criminal (adultery, witchcraft, sodomy)
- Who can hold religious office (gender equality)
- How the afterlife works (and who goes where)
- Holy sites: control them for bonuses
- Head of Faith: the Pope, Caliph, or Ecumenical Patriarch can grant claims, excommunicate, and call holy wars

#heading(level: 3)[Creating a New Faith]

The most powerful mid-game move: create your own faith.

*Why do it:*
- Make your ruler the Head of Faith (you get all that Pope money)
- Remove unpopular doctrines (no more "crime" for homosexuality)
- Add popular doctrines (Communion = money from followers, Armed Pilgrimages = free CBs)
- Make women equal (more council options)
- Make witchcraft accepted (more schemes)

*How to do it:*
1. Accumulate piety (pilgrimages, building churches, holy wars)
2. Open the faith tab > Create New Faith
3. Choose your tenets, doctrines, and Head of Faith status
4. Your new faith is a *heresy* of the old one --- expect holy wars from your former co-religionists

*Strategic tips:*
- Keep some core tenets the same to reduce opinion penalties
- Make your capital the new Head of Faith title
- Add Pastoral Isolation (reduces conversion pressure from other faiths)
- Be prepared for a massive opinion hit from your former faith

#heading(level: 3)[Holy Wars]

Different CBs unlock at different fervor levels:
- *Sanctioned invasions:* A ruler is evil and you want their whole kingdom
- *Great Holy Wars:* Called by the Head of Faith. Everyone joins. Massive war.
- *Crusades/Jihads:* The big ones. Win to place a dynasty member on a kingdom throne.

Low fervor in your faith means fewer holy wars. High fervor means more. Heresies reduce fervor significantly.

#heading(level: 2)[Culture]

#block[
*Cultural Pillars:* Heritage, Language, Aesthetics, Martial Custom. These barely change during a campaign.

*Traditions:* The active mechanics. Each culture has 3--5 traditions. You can add/remove traditions with enough prestige and cultural acceptance.

*Diverging vs Hybridizing:*
- *Diverge:* Split your culture into a new one. Useful if you've migrated somewhere new.
- *Hybridize:* Merge two cultures. Get traditions from both. Best way to get powerful combinations.

*Innovations:* Culture advances through eras (Tribal > Early Medieval > High Medieval > Late Medieval). Shared between same-culture rulers. Being ahead on innovations gives you better MaA, buildings, and laws.
]

#heading(level: 2)[Warfare]

#block[
*MaA (Men-at-Arms):* Your professional army. Culture-specific units are usually stronger. Build counter-MaA against your neighbor's composition (Spearmen beat Cavalry, Pikemen beat heavy infantry, etc.).

*Knights:* Each knight is a character. High prowess = valuable knight. Low prowess = dead knight. Equipment (from artifacts) increases prowess.

*Supply:* Armies need supply. Marching 10,000 men through the Sahara = attrition disaster. Plan routes through friendly or high-supply territory.

*Sieges:* You don't need to fight every battle. Siege weapons (trebuchets, siege towers) reduce siege time. Siege down holdings to win wars faster than fighting field battles.

*Mercenaries & Holy Orders:* Expensive but effective. Holy Orders cheaper if you share their faith. Contract them for specific wars, dismiss when done.

*Declaring war effectively:*
- Check alliance chains first (are they allied to France?)
- Declare when your MaA are fully reinforced
- Have a CB (Casus Belli) --- press claim, holy war, conquest
- Call your allies before declaring
]

#pagebreak()

#heading(level: 2)[Vassal Management]

*Deep dive: Keeping your realm from tearing itself apart.*

#heading(level: 3)[The Vassal Problem]

Your vassals want four things:
1. More land (they covet your titles)
2. More power (they want council positions, lower obligations)
3. Their culture/faith respected (wrong culture/faith = -40 opinion)
4. Your throne (factions)

Ignore any of these and factions will fire.

#heading(level: 3)[Factions]

The five major factions:
- *Liberty Faction:* Wants lower crown authority or council rights
- *Independence Faction:* Wants to leave your realm
- *Claimant Faction:* Wants someone else on your throne
- *Tax Faction:* Wants lower tax obligations
- *Cultural Faction:* Wrong-culture vassals organizing

Managing factions:
- *Prevention:* Keep vassal opinion above 0. Sway, grant artifacts, send gifts, host feasts.
- *Dread:* Execute prisoners. High dread keeps factions from forming (vassals are too scared).
- *Alliances:* Marry your children to powerful vassals' children. They can't faction against family.
- *Tit-for-tat:* When a faction fires, check its members. Can you kill the leader? Imprison them? Ally with the leader?
- *Concessions:* Sometimes giving in (lower crown authority temporarily) is better than civil war.

#heading(level: 3)[Hooks]

Hooks are *leverage* over a character. Use them to:
- Force vassals to accept higher obligations
- Force vassals to vote your way
- Prevent vassals from joining factions
- Get council position acceptance

Gain hooks through: fabricate hook scheme, secrets (expose or blackmail), swaying powerful vassals, the Spymaster's find secrets task.

#heading(level: 3)[Crown Authority]

Each crown authority level changes vassal dynamics:
- *Level 1:* Vassals can war each other. You can't revoke titles without tyranny.
- *Level 2:* You can revoke criminal titles. Vassals can't war each other.
- *Level 3:* You can designate heir (with Absolute). Vassal contracts matter more.
- *Level 4 (Absolute):* Designate any heir. No vassal war declarations at all.

Higher authority = more stability, lower vassal opinion. Balance accordingly.

#heading(level: 3)[Council Rights]

The council can have varying power:
- *No council rights:* You control everything. Low vassal happiness.
- *Council voting on war:* You need council approval to declare war. Frustrating.
- *Council voting on everything:* Very stable but very slow.

Grant council rights to powerful vassals who want them. Take them back when you're strong enough to handle the rebellion.

#heading(level: 3)[The Dread Tax]

Dread is temporary fear. When you execute prisoners, you gain dread. Dread decays over time. High dread:
- Prevents factions from forming
- Speeds up scheme detection against you
- Makes vassals less likely to refuse demands

But: high dread also makes *everyone* hate you personally. Your vassals won't rebel, but they won't help you either. Use dread as a tool, not a lifestyle.

#pagebreak()

#heading(level: 2)[The Endgame]

Once you're an emperor with stabilized succession, the game changes:

*Managing a sprawling empire:* Delegate. Give kingdoms to dynasty members. Set up stable vassal kings who like you. Use theocratic vassals (bishops) for reliable tax income.

*Stopping faction death spirals:* As your empire grows, cultural/religious differences multiply. Convert conquered territory, build temples, use multicultural traditions.

*The Mongol threat:* Around 1200, the Mongols arrive. They have 80,000+ doomstacks. Your options: submit (become a tributary), fight (needs all your allies and a good river crossing), or pray they don't come for you.

*Forming restore-decisions:*
- Restore the Roman Empire (requires Italy, Greece, large swaths of everything)
- Restore the Persian Empire (requires Persia, Mesopotamia)
- Restore the Holy Roman Empire (requires Central Europe)
- Outremer (Crusader Kingdom in the Levant)
- Unite Africa, India, etc.

*When to start a new campaign:* When your realm runs itself. When no faction can threaten you. When you've achieved your goal. CK3 is about the rise, not the plateau.

#heading(level: 2)[Staying on the Right CK3 Version]

CK3 auto-updates on Steam. This guide targets CK3 *v1.19.x*. When the game updates to a new major version:

#block[
*To stay on this guide's version:*

1. Steam Library > Right-click Crusader Kings 3 > Properties
2. Betas tab > Select the version this guide targets (e.g., "1.19 - 1.19.x")
3. Steam will downgrade CK3 to that version
4. Your mods will continue to work

*WARNING:* CK3 updates frequently (every 3--4 months). Most mods in this list target a specific CK3 version. Updating the game will likely:
- Break mods until authors release updates
- Change game mechanics referenced in this guide
- Invalidate save files

Bookmark this guide's version tag. Check back for updated versions of the guide when the game updates.
]

#heading(level: 2)[Common Mistakes & How to Avoid Them]

#table(
  columns: (auto, auto),
  [Mistake], [Solution],
  [Overextension], [Consolidate before expanding. Wait for claims. Don't conquer land your realm can't handle.],
  [Bad marriage], [Always check "Will my children be of my dynasty?" Matrilineal when needed.],
  [Wrong succession law timing], [Check succession tab before death. Disinherit early. Don't die with unmanaged succession.],
  [Ignoring factions], [Keep vassal opinion above 0. Save dread for emergencies. Pay attention to faction tab.],
  [Marrying for stats instead of traits], [Congenital traits (Genius, Beautiful, Herculean) compound over generations. Stats are temporary.],
  [Not fabricating claims], [Your Chancellor fabricates claims. Always keep this running. Always.],
  [Neglecting MaA composition], [Build MaA that counter your neighbors. Don't just build whatever looks strongest.],
  [Playing speed 5 constantly], [Slow down. Events, schemes, and opportunities fire at speed 3. You miss everything at speed 5.],
)
